VALUES ('Who are 2018’s Volunteers of the Year?', 'volunteer-ireland-awards-2018', '<p>Volunteer Ireland Awards is a major annual initiative to celebrate volunteers in Ireland. The awards – held by <a href="https://www.volunteer.ie/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">Volunteer Ireland</a> in December – shine a light on the remarkable work of volunteers around the country by honouring them on a national stage.</p>
<p>For the 2018 awards, there were more than 500 volunteers nominated. Of these, 33 were named as finalists, invited to a ceremony where 11 won in their categories and Mary Fitzgerald was <a href="https://changingireland.ie/index.php/2019/03/08/volunteer-ireland-volunteer-of-the-year/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">named as the overall Volunteer of the Year</a>.</p>
<p>One of the winners – for ‘Outstanding Group – was Pullough Community Shop, the <a href="https://changingireland.ie/index.php/2019/03/06/pullough-community-shop/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">cover story</a> for the <a href="https://bit.ly/Issue63-FebMar19" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">most recent Changing Ireland issue</a>. The other 10 winners are featured here.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<div></div>
<p><strong>Children and Youth<em> </em></strong><em>Winner: David O’Hara<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-2-2-200x300.jpeg" alt="Children and Youth winner David O''Hara" width="200" height="300" srcset="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-2-2-200x300.jpeg 200w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-2-2-768x1152.jpeg 768w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-2-2-683x1024.jpeg 683w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-2-2.jpeg 1000w" sizes="(max-width: 200px) 100vw, 200px" /></em></p>
<p>David is a mentor in <a href="https://solasproject.com/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">Solas Project</a>’s <a href="https://solasproject.com/after-schools" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">After Schools club</a> for children aged 11 to 13.</p>
<p>He is the handyman, the class clown, the extra pair of hands, the homework genius and a great support to staff.</p>
<p>Where other volunteers like to take a few weeks’ or months’ breather, David keeps going, and it is invaluable to the organisation to have at least one familiar face for the children throughout the year.</p>
<p>David recently completed a master’s in community and youth work, which ties in very well with his work.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<div><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-3-1-200x300.jpeg" alt="Health and Disability winner Marry Arrigan-Langan" width="200" height="300" srcset="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-3-1-200x300.jpeg 200w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-3-1-768x1153.jpeg 768w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-3-1-682x1024.jpeg 682w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-3-1.jpeg 999w" sizes="(max-width: 200px) 100vw, 200px" /></div>
<p><strong>Health and Disability </strong><em>Winner: Mary Arrigan-Langan</em></p>
<p>In 1981, Mary Arrigan-Langan set up <a href="https://octopusswimmingclub.com/about-us" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">Octopus Swimming Club</a> for people with physical disabilities.</p>
<p>She has been involved for 37 years, providing weekly swim sessions and hardly missing a session in all that time.</p>
<p>She also volunteers with other groups.</p>
<p>Mary is great at pushing the boundaries and getting people to be ambitious.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<div><em><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/54424690_10157182893064357_5667289546996318208_o-200x300.jpg" alt="Arts, Culture and Media winner Louise Borre" width="200" height="300" srcset="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/54424690_10157182893064357_5667289546996318208_o-200x300.jpg 200w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/54424690_10157182893064357_5667289546996318208_o-681x1024.jpg 681w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/54424690_10157182893064357_5667289546996318208_o.jpg 720w" sizes="(max-width: 200px) 100vw, 200px" /></em></div>
<p><strong>Arts, Culture and Media </strong><em>Winner: Louise Borre</em></p>
<p>Louise has been volunteering since she was seven.</p>
<p>Today, she is a <a href="https://www.giaf.ie/visit/venues/big-top" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">Big Top</a> team leader (one of four in a volunteer team of more than 600) for the <a href="https://www.giaf.ie/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">Galway International Arts Festival</a>.</p>
<p>Louise works part time (paid) at <a href="https://cosainwellness.ie/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">Cosáin Community Wellness Centre</a> and continues to volunteer many hours once her shift is done.</p>
<p>She also volunteers with a host of other groups including <a href="https://www.clifdenartsfestival.ie/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">Clifden Arts Festival</a>, <a href="https://galwaytheatrefestival.com/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">Galway Theatre Festival</a>, <a href="https://www.facebook.com/morehousesplease/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">Galway Housing Action Group</a>, <a href="https://www.pieta.ie/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">Pieta House</a> and <a href="https://www.copegalway.ie/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">Cope Galway</a>.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<div><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-4-1-199x300.jpeg" alt="Social Work and Social Inclusion winner Sheila Busher" width="199" height="300" srcset="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-4-1-199x300.jpeg 199w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-4-1-768x1157.jpeg 768w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-4-1-680x1024.jpeg 680w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-4-1.jpeg 996w" sizes="(max-width: 199px) 100vw, 199px" /></div>
<p><strong>Social Work and Social Inclusion </strong><em>Winner: Sheila Busher</em></p>
<p>Currently, Sheila organises two nights of <a href="https://food.cloud/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">FoodCloud</a> deliveries to more than 160 families who text in if they wish to receive a delivery six nights per week.</p>
<p>Whatever the hour, if someone is in trouble, homeless or vulnerable, Sheila is there for them.</p>
<p>She also has the backing of many volunteers and the local community to help her provide support.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<div><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-5-1-200x300.jpeg" alt="Campaigning and Awareness winner Laura Brennan" width="200" height="300" srcset="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-5-1-200x300.jpeg 200w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-5-1-768x1154.jpeg 768w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-5-1-681x1024.jpeg 681w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-5-1.jpeg 998w" sizes="(max-width: 200px) 100vw, 200px" /></div>
<p><strong>Campaigning and Awareness </strong><em>Winner: Laura Brennan</em></p>
<p>Laura has terminal cervical cancer and advocates in favour of the HPV vaccine. She uses the media, public speaking opportunities and social media to appeal to parents to protect their daughters from HPV and cervical cancer.</p>
<p>Laura took the initiative and contacted the <a href="https://www.hse.ie/eng/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">HSE</a> in September 2017. At the age of just 25, she was determined to tell her story to every parent in Ireland who was about to make the decision whether or not their daughter was to be vaccinated against HPV.</p>
<p>Sadly, it has been announced that Laura has succumbed to her cancer. She died today, Wednesday 20 March.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<div></div>
<div><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-6-300x199.jpeg" alt="Sports and Recreation winner Sinead Kelly" width="300" height="199" srcset="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-6-300x199.jpeg 300w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-6-768x509.jpeg 768w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-6-1024x679.jpeg 1024w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-6-1080x716.jpeg 1080w" sizes="(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px" /></div>
<p><strong>Sport and Recreation </strong><em>Winner: Sinead Kelly</em></p>
<p>Four years ago, Raphoe Town FC had fewer then 10 girls playing football. Sinead took the initiative to set up and organise a Soccer Sisters programme and kept going even when hospitalised.</p>
<p>Sinead has positioned the club in the heart of the community, building strong and positive links with local schools and clubs. There are now more than 30 girls playing on the team.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<div></div>
<div><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-7-199x300.jpeg" alt="Volunteer Manager of the Year winner Sara Murphy" width="199" height="300" srcset="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-7-199x300.jpeg 199w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-7-768x1157.jpeg 768w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-7-680x1024.jpeg 680w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-7.jpeg 996w" sizes="(max-width: 199px) 100vw, 199px" /></div>
<p><strong>Volunteer Manager of the Year </strong><em>Winner: Sara Murphy</em></p>
<p><a href="https://www.vsac.ie/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">Victim Support at Court </a>is a voluntary service that provides court accompaniment and support to victims of crime. Sara manages victim support in the Criminal Courts of Justice, which deal mainly with murder and rape trials.</p>
<p>Sara’s supportive, warm and empathetic nature, as well as her knowledge of the court system, led to her being offered a part-time role managing the volunteers in the Criminal Courts of Justice and helping with training of new volunteers.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-8-300x200.jpeg" alt="Safety and Emergency Services winner Nancy White" width="300" height="200" srcset="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-8-300x200.jpeg 300w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-8-768x511.jpeg 768w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-8-1024x681.jpeg 1024w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-8-1080x719.jpeg 1080w" sizes="(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px" /><strong>Safety and Emergency Services </strong><em>Winner: Nancy White</em></p>
<p>Nancy single-handedly brought together a group of volunteers and set up the <a href="https://www.facebook.com/lorrhacommunityrespondergroup/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">Lorrha Community Responder Group</a>.</p>
<p>She also gives an incredible amount of time to groups.</p>
<p>Under her leadership, five new defibrillators have been acquired for the Lorrha area, and the group has already responded to more than 30<br />
emergency calls under the direction of the National Ambulance Service.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<div></div>
<div><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-9-200x300.jpeg" alt="Animals and Environment winner Maeve O''Donoghue" width="200" height="300" srcset="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-9-200x300.jpeg 200w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-9-768x1154.jpeg 768w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-9-681x1024.jpeg 681w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-9.jpeg 998w" sizes="(max-width: 200px) 100vw, 200px" /></div>
<p><strong>Animals and Environment </strong><em>Winner: Maeve O’Donoghue</em></p>
<p>Maeve volunteers in the local dog pound and seeks, wherever possible, to reunite pets with owners or have them rehomed.</p>
<p>By law, dogs who are in the pound only have to be kept for a certain number of days and then they can be rehomed or put to sleep.</p>
<p>She works closely with the dog warden and does her utmost to get every dog out of the pound alive. She visits most days, takes photos of strays and posts the pictures to the <a href="https://www.facebook.com/MullingarDogShelter/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">Mullingar Dog Shelter</a>Facebook page.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-10-300x199.jpeg" alt="Community winner Cormac Sertutxa" width="300" height="199" srcset="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-10-300x199.jpeg 300w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-10-768x510.jpeg 768w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-10-1024x680.jpeg 1024w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/0-10-1080x717.jpeg 1080w" sizes="(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px" /><strong>Community </strong><em>Winner: Cormac Sertutxa</em></p>
<p>Cormac is one of the <a href="https://www.redcross.ie/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">Irish Red Cross</a>’s busiest volunteers. As the health and social care officer responsible for developing services in Co Kerry, he spends every day focused on his role.</p>
<p>Cormac has also volunteered with other organisations, including <a href="https://www.facebook.com/southwestmotorsportrescue/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">Southwest Motorsport Rescue Unit</a>, <a href="https://www.cycleagainstsuicide.com/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">Cycle Against Suicide</a>, <a href="https://www.facebook.com/Killorglincfr" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">Killorglin Community First Responders</a>, <a href="https://www.facebook.com/Keel-Castlemaine-Youth-Club-777251198993746/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">Keel Castlemaine Youth Club</a> and Killorglin KDYS’s<br />
Neighbourhood Project.</p>
<p><strong>Updated, 1pm, 21 March 2019: </strong>This article was updated as news of Laura Brennan&#8217;s passing filtered through to the <em>Changing Ireland</em> offices. The entire team here is saddened to hear of the Brennan family&#8217;s loss and, indeed, Ireland&#8217;s loss. Thank you, Laura, for all you have done.</p>
<p><strong><em>Body photos: Volunteer Ireland</em></strong></p>', 'Last year (2018), the 11th annual Volunteer Ireland awards honoured a dozen people for their voluntary contributions, but who took home what prizes?', 'https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2019/03/5.jpeg', 'Attendees at the 2018 Volunteer Ireland Awards ceremony pose for selfies. Image: Volunteer Ireland', '[{"slug":"activism-volunteering","title":"Activism & Volunteering"}]', '[{"slug":"awards-recognition","title":"awards and recognition"},{"slug":"volunteer-ireland","title":"Volunteer Ireland"},{"slug":"volunteering","title":"volunteering"},{"slug":"volunteers","title":"volunteers"}]', '', 247406, 1, '2019-03-20T12:32:03Z', '2019-09-13T13:49:08Z', '2019-03-20T12:32:03Z')
